@@ -0,0 +1,73 @@
+# P06 - Flow executor semantics and captured effects
+
+This directory is the independent hand-off for P06. It characterizes `pkg/flow` as a typed operational executor, adds explicit effect/locality contracts, separates definition/stage/operation/attempt identity, introduces offline attempt capture and replay, and supplies a metamorphic conformance harness.
+
+The design preserves the repository's existing boundary: **`flow` owns execution mechanics; the calling Go program owns workflow structure.** No DAG scheduler or workflow DSL is introduced.
+
+## What was implemented
+
+- `pkg/effectlog`: concurrency-safe capture/replay of external attempt outcomes.
+- `pkg/flow`: explicit `Contract`, trace identities, `flow-report/v2`, `operation-trace/v1`, capture integration with `captured-effect-event/v1`, and `Snapshot` for collection-global operations.
+- `pkg/flowtest`: semantic projections and metamorphic comparison of executor variants.
+- `standalone/`: a standard-library executable model independent of rag-ttc internals.
+- `adapter/`: conformance and fault-injection tests against the actual `flow` implementation.
+- `fixtures/` and `schemas/`: neutral hand-off artifacts for later projects.
+- Generation and embedding adapters now declare read-only effects and provide JSON capture codecs.
+
+## Primary result
+
+A policy change is transparent only under a declared precondition. In particular:
+
+- worker count is transparent for item-local operations when results are restored by input identity;
+- caching is transparent only with a complete semantic key and schema-compatible value;
+- batching is transparent only when it preserves one correctly aligned result per logical input;
+- retry is transparent for pure, read-only, or genuinely idempotent operations under a stable request;
+- capture makes attempts replayable, but **does not** make an ambiguous non-idempotent mutation exactly-once;
+- `Barrier` is temporal only; global top-k or normalization requires `Snapshot` access to the complete collection;
+- budgets, skip policy, and fail-fast behavior intentionally affect completion and must be explicit in the report.
+
+## Run the offline demonstration
+
+From the repository root:
+
+```bash
+./research/p06-flow-executor-semantics-effects/demo.sh
+```
+
+The script uses the declared module and toolchain when available. With an older offline Go installation, it falls back to the bundled GOPATH compatibility harness for the P06 target packages. The harness is validation-only and does not replace production dependencies.
+
+The script runs:
+
+- race-enabled unit and conformance tests;
+- `go vet` over P06 target packages;
+- 720 completion-order schedules;
+- 128 contiguous batch partitions;
+- actual `flow.Bulk` batch sizes 1 through 8;
+- report identity, capture/replay, snapshot-global, and unsafe-retry scenarios;
+- schema validation when Python `jsonschema` is installed.
+
+## Directory map
+
+```text
+adapter/        rag-ttc flow adapter, fault tests, and benchmarks
+assets/         source diagrams used by the report
+cmd/            offline experiment and artifact generators
+compat/         validation-only GOPATH stubs for the unavailable toolchain
+fixtures/       neutral adversarial scenarios
+results/raw/    machine-generated test, benchmark, trace, report, and capture data
+schemas/        JSON Schemas for P06 interchange artifacts
+scripts/        artifact validation utilities
+standalone/     executable semantic specification
+api.md          public API and integration examples
+contract-catalog.md
+                combinator-by-combinator transparency conditions
+design.md       final semantic model and rejected alternatives
+report.md       methods, results, limits, and recommendations
+results.json    program-level machine-readable result
+```
+
+## Validation status
+
+All 102 selected P06 target-package test functions pass under the race detector and `go vet` under the offline Go 1.23.2 compatibility harness. The production generation and embedding packages compile in that harness. Their existing tests use APIs added after Go 1.23 and therefore require the repository's declared Go 1.26.5 toolchain. The complete module suite was not run in this environment because the declared toolchain and modules could not be downloaded.
+
+See `report.md` for exact claim levels. No benchmark number is treated as a semantic guarantee.
