index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..5c125e9c3019639d5c121a1aa9c1343dcfc34b57
--- /dev/null
+++ b/research/p06-flow-executor-semantics-effects/contract-catalog.md
@@ -0,0 +1,190 @@
+# Operational contract catalog
+
+This catalog states the meaning of each major `pkg/flow` and `pkg/execution` mechanism. "Transparent" means that, after projecting away operational history, the variant has the same aligned semantic outcome as its baseline. Transparency is always conditional.
+
+## Semantic outcome used by P06
+
+For input position `i`, the semantic outcome is one of:
+
+```text
+Success(value)
+Quarantined(error class, error message)
+Skipped
+RunStopped(stop reason, error)
+```
+
+The default conformance projection ignores worker order, timestamps, run/stage/operation/attempt IDs, cache hit/miss state, retry count, and meter timing. It does not ignore a changed value, changed item alignment, changed quarantine/skip status, or a changed terminal error contract.
+
+## Core step and composition contracts
+
+| Mechanism | Intended meaning | Transparent when | Known counterexample or caveat | Evidence |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `Step[I,O]` | One typed operation applied to logical inputs. | `Do` satisfies its declared effect and locality contract; identity describes all output-affecting inputs. | Hidden mutable state, incomplete keys, or cross-item behavior in an item-local `Do`. | Contract validation plus adapter tests. |
+| `Run` | Execute a step or flattened pipeline and return one result aligned to each declared input. | All stages satisfy their contracts; no intentional stop policy fires. | Budget exhaustion, cancellation, preflight refusal, fail-fast failure. These are explicit stopped runs, not successful empty results. | Existing order tests, P06 reports, budget fixture. |
+| `Pipe2/3/4` | Typed function composition of stages. | Each stage is lawful and any cross-item dependency uses an explicit snapshot boundary. | A hidden global operation can see different subsets under streaming or batching. | Existing pipe tests and snapshot counterexample. |
+| `Barrier` | Wait until every upstream result is available before starting the stage. | Used only as a temporal ordering requirement. | It still calls `Do` once per item and therefore cannot implement global top-k or normalization. | `TestTemporalBarrierIsNotCollectionAccess`. |
+| `Snapshot` | Call one operation with the complete declared collection and return one aligned output per input. | `DoAll` returns exactly one correctly aligned result per input; its effect contract is valid. | Cardinality-changing compaction is not representable as aligned `Snapshot` output and belongs outside this API. | Snapshot implementation and global top-one tests. |
+| `OnResult` | Observe successful results in completion order. | Observer is operational only, or is itself idempotent/order-independent and its failure semantics are accepted. | Writing order-sensitive state in `OnResult` makes worker scheduling observable; observer errors fail the run. | Source contract; not claimed universally transparent. |
+| `Ledger` | Append run trace events. | Ledger is available and does not fail; event ordering is interpreted by sequence IDs. | Ledger errors deliberately fail the run. Concurrent append order can differ from semantic item order. | Existing ledger tests and sample trace. |
+
+## Effect contracts
+
+| Effect kind | Meaning | Automatic retry | Capture/replay | Required discipline |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `unknown` | Legacy step with no audited effect claim. | Allowed for compatibility, but no transparency claim follows. | Optional only if capture codec is configured. | Migrate to an explicit effect before relying on conformance results. |
+| `pure` | Output depends only on declared input; no external observation or mutation. | Yes. | Usually unnecessary, but possible. | No time, randomness, hidden state, or mutable global dependency unless included in the semantic input. |
+| `read-only` | Observes an external system without mutating it. | Yes, conditionally. | Recommended for provider/index calls. | Stable request; repeated observations are accepted as the same contract or captured for exact replay. |
+| `idempotent` | May mutate, but repeating the same logical request has no additional externally visible effect. | Yes, conditionally. | Supported. | External system must enforce idempotency under the same semantic/idempotency key. |
+| `captured` | External attempt outcome must pass through `effectlog`. | Yes for the captured/read-like operation represented by the adapter. | Required. | Capture codec and recorder are mandatory. This is not a claim that arbitrary mutation is exactly-once. |
+| `non-idempotent` | Repetition may produce an additional external mutation. | No; `Attempts > 1` is rejected. | Capture can record/replay, but does not make the first live run safe to retry. | Use a provider transaction, idempotency key, or one attempt. |
+
+### Retry law
+
+For a compliant retryable operation `f` and a stable logical request `x`:
+
+```text
+admit(first successful attempt of f(x)) == admit(single successful f(x))
+```
+
+This law says nothing about wall-clock time or the number of provider calls. It is false for an ambiguous mutation that commits and then loses its response.
+
+## Identity contracts
+
+| Identity | Scope | Derivation | Semantic role |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `RunID` | One top-level execution. | Supplied or generated. | Trace only. It must not change capture replay lookup. |
+| `StepID` | Stable step definition and version. | Domain/version separated hash. | Defines the operation family and participates in capture identity. |
+| `StageID` | One placement of a step in one run. | `hash(RunID, ordinal, StepID)`. | Prevents repeated same-name or same-definition placements from conflating. |
+| `OperationID` | One logical request within a stage. | `hash(StageID, semantic key or position key)`. | Groups duplicate input positions and all attempts for one request. |
+| `AttemptID` | One retry attempt. | `hash(OperationID, attempt number)`. | Distinguishes transient failure and later success. |
+| `BatchID` | One physical grouped provider call. | `hash(StageID, batch ordinal or member identity)`. | Relates physical batching to logical operations; not semantic output identity. |
+| `Name` | Human-readable label. | Caller supplied. | Presentation and legacy aggregation only. Never a unique key. |
+
+The v2 report validates stage derivation, attempt derivation, uniqueness, and cross-links. It cannot reconstruct the local semantic key used to derive every operation ID, so operation hash verification remains the producer's responsibility.
+
+## Cache contract
+
+A cache hit is transparent when all of the following hold:
+
+1. the key includes every value that may change the admitted output or error contract;
+2. the stored schema and decoder match the current step version;
+3. the store returns the exact admitted value associated with that key;
+4. hidden external state is either outside the claimed semantics or represented in the key;
+5. cache failure is handled according to the declared contract.
+
+`flow.Store` fails closed on load/decode errors. It does not silently return a zero value or automatically call live work after corruption. That behavior is operationally stricter than a fallback cache and is visible to callers.
+
+Cache and capture differ:
+
+| Question | Result cache | Effect capture |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| What is stored? | Final successful admitted output. | Every attempted response or classified error. |
+| Lookup identity | Cache identity produced by `Step.Identity`. | `StepID + semantic key + attempt number`. |
+| Does it preserve retry history? | No. | Yes. |
+| Can it avoid the whole operation? | Yes. | Yes, one attempt at a time. |
+| Is a hit metered as fresh work? | No. | No. |
+| Does it make mutation exactly-once? | No. | No. |
+
+## Worker and ordering contract
+
+`Run` may complete items concurrently but writes each result into the slot associated with the original input index. Therefore worker count is transparent when:
+
+- the step is item-local;
+- the operation does not depend on completion order;
+- output identity is not assigned by arrival time;
+- `OnResult` is not treated as semantic state, or its own operation is lawful;
+- a shared external system does not expose schedule-dependent mutation.
+
+The standalone model exhaustively checked all `6! = 720` completion schedules for six inputs. The actual adapter also executes gated completion schedules and verifies aligned outputs and valid report identity.
+
+## Bulk contract
+
+`Bulk(base, doBulk, batchSize)` changes physical calls but is intended to preserve each logical item result.
+
+Required preconditions:
+
+1. `doBulk` receives items in the declared order;
+2. it returns exactly one output for every input;
+3. output `j` belongs to input `j`, or an explicit mapping is validated;
+4. the bulk operation is observationally equivalent to applying the base operation to each item;
+5. duplicate-key coalescing is valid under the base semantic identity;
+6. retrying the physical batch is lawful for every effect it contains;
+7. one physical failure maps to logical item failures according to the declared policy;
+8. metering describes fresh logical results or physical calls consistently and is documented.
+
+The runtime detects response-length mismatch. It cannot detect this same-length violation:
+
+```text
+input:  [1, 2, 3, 4]
+output: [16, 9, 4, 1] // reversed squares
+```
+
+The metamorphic suite detects it by comparison with the per-item baseline. Provider adapters should prefer explicit item IDs over positional trust where supported.
+
+## `Batched` with repair contract
+
+`Batched` groups inputs, sends each group to `DoAll`, splits the group response, and routes missing or unparsable items to a repair step.
+
+Transparency requires:
+
+- each input index appears in at most one group;
+- group construction is deterministic or semantically irrelevant;
+- `Split` maps every recognized response to the correct input index;
+- the repair step has the same output semantics as a successful group result;
+- group and repair cache identities are complete;
+- group retries are lawful;
+- shared budgets are intentionally coupled;
+- an omitted result is distinguishable from a valid zero value.
+
+`Batched` is a barrier stage because it must collect enough inputs to form groups. It is not itself a general collection algebra: the output is still aligned per input and repair semantics remain explicit.
+
+## Failure policy contract
+
+| Policy | Semantic effect | Transparency claim |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Fail fast | Stops the run after a terminal failure and cancels pending work. | Not transparent to a complete successful run; correct when fail-fast is part of the API. |
+| Quarantine | Returns an explicit per-item error record and lets other items continue. | Transparent only to a semantics that includes quarantine as a first-class outcome. |
+| Skip | Marks the item skipped and continues. | Intentionally loses a value; never transparent to a complete map. |
+
+Transient/fatal/data classification determines which failures retry and which failure mode applies. A classifier is therefore part of operational semantics and must be versioned when its behavior affects admitted results.
+
+## Admission, budget, rate, and preflight contracts
+
+| Mechanism | Contract |
+| --- | --- |
+| Resource budget | Finite, atomic admission count. Exhaustion is `StatusStopped` with `budget-exhausted`; it is not a successful empty result. |
+| Rate limiter | Delays admission. Transparent only if delay does not trigger deadline/cancellation or change the external observation contract. |
+| `Chain` | Applies limiters in order. Earlier refusal prevents later limiter side effects. |
+| Preflight | Refuses before item one when coverage, pricing, or maximum-cost rules fail. This intentionally changes run availability, not item meaning. |
+| Shared options | Steps declaring the same resource draw from one budget. This is deliberate coupling and must be visible in the plan/report. |
+| Partial budget | A stopped run may have attempted a subset of operations. P06 treats that subset as trace data, not as a complete semantic result. |
+
+## Report and trace contract
+
+`flow-report/v2` has two views:
+
+- `Stages` and `Operations`: authoritative identity-preserving records;
+- `Steps[name]`: legacy, display-name-aggregated compatibility view.
+
+A complete report states `status=completed` and `stop_reason=completed`. A stopped report names one of preflight refusal, budget exhaustion, cancellation, deadline, or failure. `Validate` checks the identity hierarchy and references, but not provider truth.
+
+`operation-trace/v1` is append-oriented operational history. The event `sequence` supplies a run-local total order even when concurrent ledger calls arrive in another physical order. Consumers must not use timestamps or event sequence as semantic item identity.
+
+## Metamorphic comparisons
+
+A variant is accepted as transparent only when:
+
+```text
+project(baseline(inputs)) == project(variant(inputs))
+```
+
+where `project` removes trace-only differences. The test must also inspect the report to verify that expected operational differences actually occurred; equal outputs from a variant that accidentally skipped its cache or retry path are insufficient evidence.
+
+The supplied harness includes positive and negative controls:
+
+- sequential versus parallel pure operation: positive;
+- item versus aligned bulk: positive;
+- item versus reversed bulk: negative;
+- first live captured retry versus offline replay: positive;
+- item-local top-one versus snapshot-global top-one: negative;
+- non-idempotent retry: rejected before execution.
